    <section class="shell section--roomy">
      <div class="sec-head reveal">
        <span class="num">02 / The problem</span>
        <div>
          <h2>A dashboard that<br />forgot its reader.</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-prose reveal" data-delay="1">
        <p>
          Arcline had been built feature by feature for twelve years. The main advisor
          surface had become a shelf of <strong>nineteen nested tables</strong>: holdings,
          performance, positions, allocations, trade blotter, cash activity. It ran. But
          when we sat with advisors during review prep, they were
          <strong>printing it out</strong>, highlighting with a pen, and building their
          client conversation from the margins.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two things were happening. First, the information model had drifted: every new
          feature got its own tab. Second &mdash; and this was the important one &mdash;
          the interface treated all information as equally important, so the advisor had
          to do the hierarchy work every single review.
        </p>
      </div>

