    <section class="shell section--roomy">
      <div class="sec-head reveal">
        <span class="num">03 / The approach</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Rebuild around<br />the review, not the record.</h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-prose reveal" data-delay="1">
        <p>
          I ran sixteen hours of interviews across twelve advisors &mdash; three early-career,
          six mid, three senior &mdash; in the weeks leading up to their quarterly reviews.
          We watched the whole arc: prep, client meeting, follow-ups. The findings were
          uncomfortably consistent.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The review is a story, not a snapshot.</strong> Advisors pieced together a
            narrative across five tabs. The product made them do that stitching every time.</li>
          <li><strong>Events beat numbers.</strong> A rebalance, a life event, a market move &mdash;
            these are what clients remember. Positions are evidence, not the point.</li>
          <li><strong>Default to defensible.</strong> Advisors worked slower than expected
            because they were double-checking the interface, not the data.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The redesign collapsed the nineteen tables into a single
          <strong>household timeline</strong>. Every visible row is an event you can point
          at in a client meeting: a deposit, a contribution, a tax harvest, a model change.
          Numbers live inside the events, not the other way around.
        </p>
      </div>

